{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793413363,"nonce":"6+bNvVhVQ2Z3dKSUz1uk+EambHE=","title":"LSP re-analyses a document on every request when a library rewrites a file its macros read","message":"Seen in RayDE on pocket-studio (src/ui/BoardApp.hx, Ashui 0.2.2): HXX tag hovers each waited a full analysis (75–144 s on the Mac), and reopening the file never used its saved .bidx index; ordinary declarations hovered quickly from the declaration-only partial index.\n\nCause, from RAYZOR_LSP_PROFILE (lsp-stale-macro-input):\n- Ashui compiled its user-agent stylesheet into $TMPDIR/ashui-css/\u003cmd5(path)\u003e.bcss on every compile, through an external blinc-css process.\n- Every Ashui build on the machine rewrote that file, including 21 Haxe compiler servers, RayDE and other LSPs, at any version. Rayzor recorded the file as a macro input and found it changed, sometimes with different content.\n- Each request then started a new analysis, and a snapshot with a stale input was never served or reused.\n- Separately, requests that arrived before the worker had configured the editor cache went to the worker, whose loop can stay inside one analysis for minutes; so a reopened file waited even with a usable index.\n\nFixed:\n- 5420cee6: a snapshot whose only changed inputs are files macros read (not Haxe sources) answers at once, and the document is re-analysed in the background. A changed source still waits.\n- 2028ce9b: the front holds messages, in order, until the cache is configured (at most 3 s), and the worker also answers from such a snapshot.\n- Ashui a1090d2 (main) makes the cache content-addressed and atomic. pocket-studio still uses 0.2.2.\n\nMeasured on pocket-studio after the shared cache changed: a new session answered its first hover in 85 ms (was 75–110 s).\nNot addressed: the cold first analysis itself (75–110 s) is 577b194.","files":null}]}